## Experiment 8: Optimal Binary Search Tree

**Q1. What is a Binary Search Tree?**

Ans: A binary tree in which the keys in the left subtree are smaller than the root and the keys in the right subtree are greater than the root.

**Q2. What is an Optimal Binary Search Tree?**

Ans: A Binary Search Tree constructed to minimize the expected or weighted search cost.

**Q3. What is the full form of OBST?**

Ans: Optimal Binary Search Tree.

**Q4. Which algorithm technique is used in this experiment?**

Ans: Dynamic Programming.

**Q5. What is the objective of an Optimal Binary Search Tree?**

Ans: To minimize the total weighted search cost of the given keys.

**Q6. What is search frequency?**

Ans: The number of times a particular key is searched.

**Q7. Why are search frequencies important in OBST?**

Ans: They determine the contribution of each key to the total search cost.

**Q8. Where are frequently searched keys generally placed?**

Ans: Closer to the root of the tree.

**Q9. What is the difference between BST and OBST?**

Ans: A normal BST follows the binary search ordering property, whereas an OBST additionally minimizes the weighted search cost.

**Q10. What is the difference between OBST and AVL Tree?**

Ans: An OBST minimizes weighted search cost, whereas an AVL Tree maintains height balance.

**Q11. Is an Optimal Binary Search Tree always balanced?**

Ans: No. Its structure depends on the search frequencies.

**Q12. What is Dynamic Programming?**

Ans: An algorithm design technique that solves overlapping subproblems and stores their results.

**Q13. Why is Dynamic Programming used in OBST?**

Ans: Because OBST has overlapping subproblems and optimal substructure.

**Q14. What is optimal substructure?**

Ans: A property in which an optimal solution can be constructed using optimal solutions of smaller subproblems.

**Q15. What does cost[i][j] represent?**

Ans: The minimum weighted search cost for keys from index i to index j.

**Q16. What does root[i][j] represent?**

Ans: The index of the optimal root for keys from index i to index j.

**Q17. What is the recurrence relation of OBST?**

Ans: cost[i][j] = sum of frequencies + minimum of left subtree cost and right subtree cost combined over all possible roots.

**Q18. What is the cost of an empty subtree?**

Ans: Zero.

**Q19. What is the cost of a subtree containing a single key?**

Ans: The search frequency of that key.

**Q20. Why is every key considered as a possible root?**

Ans: To identify the root that produces the minimum total search cost.

**Q21. What is the time complexity of the standard OBST algorithm?**

Ans: O(N^3).

**Q22. What is the space complexity of OBST using Dynamic Programming?**

Ans: O(N^2).

**Q23. Why is the time complexity O(N^3)?**

Ans: The algorithm considers O(N^2) intervals and up to N possible roots for each interval.

**Q24. What is a prefix sum?**

Ans: An array storing cumulative sums of elements.

**Q25. Why are prefix sums used in our program?**

Ans: To calculate the sum of frequencies within any interval in constant time.

**Q26. What is the search cost formula for a key?**

Ans: Search frequency multiplied by the level of the key, with the root at level one.

**Q27. How is the total weighted search cost calculated?**

Ans: By adding the weighted search costs of all keys.

**Q28. What is the level of the root in our calculation?**

Ans: One.

**Q29. What is the minimum search cost in our first test case?**

Ans: 142.

**Q30. Which key is selected as the root in our first test case?**

Ans: 20.

**Q31. What are the search frequencies in our first test case?**

Ans: 34, 8 and 50 for keys 10, 12 and 20 respectively.

**Q32. Can multiple Optimal Binary Search Trees exist?**

Ans: Yes. Different tree structures may have the same minimum weighted search cost.

**Q33. Can OBST contain duplicate keys in our implementation?**

Ans: No. The program requires distinct keys in ascending order.

**Q34. Can search frequencies be negative?**

Ans: No. Search frequencies represent nonnegative counts or weights.

**Q35. What happens when all search frequencies are equal?**

Ans: The optimal tree minimizes the sum of key levels and generally has a balanced structure.

**Q36. Does selecting the most frequent key as the root always guarantee an optimal tree?**

Ans: No. The search costs of the remaining subtrees must also be considered.

**Q37. Which Dynamic Programming approach is used in our program?**

Ans: Bottom-up Dynamic Programming using tabulation.

**Q38. What is tabulation?**

Ans: A Dynamic Programming technique that calculates smaller subproblems first and stores their results in a table.

**Q39. What is the purpose of the printTree() function?**

Ans: To reconstruct and display the optimal tree using the stored root indices.

**Q40. What is a successful search?**

Ans: A search operation in which the requested key exists in the tree.

**Q41. What is an unsuccessful search?**

Ans: A search operation in which the requested key does not exist in the tree.

**Q42. Does our implementation consider unsuccessful search frequencies?**

Ans: No. It considers only successful search frequencies.

**Q43. Can the general OBST problem include unsuccessful searches?**

Ans: Yes. The general formulation can include probabilities of both successful and unsuccessful searches.

**Q44. What is the difference between OBST and Huffman Coding?**

Ans: OBST maintains the sorted ordering of search keys, whereas Huffman Coding minimizes weighted encoding length without requiring BST ordering.

**Q45. What is Knuth's optimization?**

Ans: A technique that can reduce the standard OBST Dynamic Programming time complexity from O(N^3) to O(N^2).

**Q46. Does OBST minimize the worst-case search time?**

Ans: Not necessarily. It minimizes the weighted or expected search cost.

**Q47. What is the main advantage of OBST?**

Ans: It reduces the average search cost when search frequencies are known.

**Q48. What is the main disadvantage of OBST?**

Ans: Construction requires additional computation and may need to be repeated when search frequencies change.

**Q49. What are the applications of OBST?**

Ans: Dictionary lookup, compiler symbol tables, static lookup tables and frequently accessed records.

**Q50. Why is OBST considered an optimization problem?**

Ans: Because it selects a tree structure that minimizes the total weighted search cost.
